[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_2, web_server_3
diagnosis_text:
The issue is at the application layer on the web servers, not the network. DNS resolution and IP connectivity are healthy: from host_1_1_1_1, web2.local resolves to 10.200.0.5 and web3.local to 10.200.0.6, both pingable. HTTP on port 80 works and returns 200 OK for both sites (curl -I http://web2.local and http://web3.local succeed immediately). However, HTTPS on port 443 fails: curl -skI https://web2.local and https://web3.local both fail, and a TCP probe to port 443 shows the port is closed. On the servers themselves, netstat shows only 0.0.0.0:80 listening and no listener on 443 for both web_server_2 and web_server_3.

Mechanism: many browsers upgrade to HTTPS (via HSTS or user/browser defaults). Because web_server_2 and web_server_3 do not provide an HTTPS listener, HTTPS connections fail, which appears as pages “spinning” or not loading for those sites, while other internal pages that are accessed over HTTP continue to work.

Localization: Apache/HTTPD configuration on web_server_2 (10.200.0.5) and web_server_3 (10.200.0.6), specifically the absence of a TLS/HTTPS virtual host and Listen 443.

Root cause: HTTPS is not configured or enabled on web_server_2 and web_server_3.

Corrective fix: Configure and enable HTTPS on both servers (install certificates/keys, enable ssl module, add VirtualHost *:443 with SSLEngine on, and Listen 443), then restart Apache. As a temporary workaround, direct users to use http:// explicitly or clear HSTS for these hostnames. No network routing changes are required. 
=== END ===